#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152893} :activity/id #uuid "49b1c4fb-702c-52f3-92db-25ef7892442a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048623 :topic/id #uuid "af7275d5-2e25-54b0-b796-be667c56a628" :topic/math-academy-id 2673} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153446 :tutorial/content "Recall that two integers $a$ and $b$ are *congruent modulo* $n$ if they have the same remainder when divided by $n$.\n\nMoreover, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $a-b$.\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a-b = kn\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\nA convenient property of congruences is that we can add and subtract on both sides, just as with regular equations.\n\nConsider, for instance, the following true statement:\n\n$$\n22\\equiv 10 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nThe **addition property** allows us to add an integer to both sides. For example, adding $2$ to both sides gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}22 + 2 & ≡10 + 2 & (mod12) \\\\ 24 & ≡12 & (mod12)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich is also a true statement since $24$ and $12$ both have a remainder of zero when divided by $12$.\n\nSimilarly, the **subtraction property** allows us to subtract an integer from both sides. For example, subtracting $9$ from both sides of our original statement gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}22 - 9 & ≡10 - 9 & (mod12) \\\\ 13 & ≡1 & (mod12)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $13$ and $1$ both have a remainder of $1$ when divided by $12$.\n\nIn general, the addition and subtraction properties state the following:\n\n$$\na≡b⟺a + c≡b + c(modn); a≡b⟺a - c≡b - c(modn)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4cfdbd62-6b93-54bb-ad88-40ac101afaad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9012 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "59df1347-5830-5a1b-a7dd-84190b29fa90" :step/math-academy-id 129882 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152894}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152894 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151834 :question/id #uuid "38f80c3a-6e6e-503d-a015-7040edc622bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-14580"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2755c3f-e76f-5cf9-a498-333e7e98ea8d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049004}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152473}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Addition and Subtraction Properties"} :step/id #uuid "f23b3d68-c651-533b-847d-d2baf963aeb1" :step/math-academy-id 129883 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152895}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153447 :tutorial/content "According to the definition of modular congruence, we have that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = b+ kn\n$$\n\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting $b=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid a \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn\n$$\n\nThis gives rise to the following important fact:\n\n> *Any integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $n$.*\n\nFor example, the numbers $10$, $-25$, and $75$ are all are all multiples of ${\\color{blue}5}$, so they are all congruent to $0$ modulo ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}10 &= 2 \\cdot 5≡0 & (mod5) \\\\ -25|= (-5) \\cdot 5≡0 & (mod5) \\\\ 75|= 15 \\cdot 5≡0 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "28b78d8b-aded-5d42-adda-0c07f20db3fa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9013 :tutorial/title "Integers Congruent to Zero"} :step/id #uuid "4b3f7cd7-6397-59b0-999d-ac2990f23ca1" :step/math-academy-id 129887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152059 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151836 :question/id #uuid "e379f1ee-3de3-56ad-8866-d9189f892704" :question/math-academy-id "e-14582"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "489ba3cf-c7da-5766-9638-2b5c5429d04c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152808}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integers That Are Congruent to Zero Modulo N"} :step/id #uuid "101ac49e-3080-5f2b-9120-2dde89075a5c" :step/math-academy-id 129888 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153450 :tutorial/content "Let's try to simplify the right-hand side of the following congruence:\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 102 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\n$$\n\nBy \"simplify,\" we mean that we wish to transform the number on the right-hand side to be the smallest possible positive integer. In other words, we want to write\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < 5\n$$\n\nWhen we add $0$ to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing $0$ as an integer multiple of $n$. This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:{\\color{black}n})$.\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that ${\\color{blue}5} \\cdot (-20) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}5})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡102 & (mod5) \\\\ x + 0 & ≡102 + 0 & (mod5) \\\\ x & ≡102 + 5 \\cdot (-20) & (mod5) \\\\ x & ≡102 - 100 & (mod5) \\\\ x & ≡2 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x \\equiv 102\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$ is equivalent to $x \\equiv 2\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$.\n\nOur answer is correct because $102$ and $2$ both have a remainder of $2$ when divided by $5$, and\n\n$$\n0 \\leq 2 < 5\n$$\n\n**Note:** By the division algorithm, there is exactly one value of $r$ that satisfies\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < n\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "abbf19fd-de43-5dd3-8007-2e6b8afda54a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9016 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Congruences Using Integers Congruent to Zero"} :step/id #uuid "d7cc3e43-fac0-5489-9b4f-24b9546ae859" :step/math-academy-id 129891 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152060 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151839 :question/id #uuid "e92680c1-e40a-521d-b5b5-16a801e016ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-14585"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7faa3c23-fa98-5c95-9c09-f0d489dbd472" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152482}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Congruences"} :step/id #uuid "a66be5cb-3b33-5cd4-969f-cc79d99883f9" :step/math-academy-id 129892 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152061 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151863 :question/id #uuid "1da5794f-02be-5a46-9450-d4b5611ee5a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14646"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea9b0644-090a-52a4-bc21-18fee98acbd3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152494}] :knowledge-point/title "Date Problems"} :step/id #uuid "2687d1c2-403e-50c5-9c11-c27eb3dec9c3" :step/math-academy-id 129896 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153472 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we've been working with the so-called addition property of modular arithmetic.\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the addition property by writing $a\\equiv b$ in terms of divisibility:\n\n$$\na≡b(modn) \\mid ⟺a - b = kn; ⟺(a + c) - (b + c) = kn; ⟺(a + c)≡(b + c)(modn)\n$$\n\nThe subtraction property is proved similarly:\n\n$$\na≡b(modn) \\mid ⟺a - b = kn; ⟺(a - c) - (b - c) = kn; ⟺(a - c)≡(b - c)(modn)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "99c9429f-482b-52c6-a194-aad63808d792" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9123 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Addition and Subtraction Properties"} :step/id #uuid "717075d8-24f1-5d84-948e-a60b9b4033eb" :step/math-academy-id 137432}] :activity/title "The Addition Property of Modular Arithmetic" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152340}]}
